[Fact:spectrumcomputing:Spectrum Computing]
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/6431/ZX-Spectrum/Heroes_of_Karn"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Heroes Of Karn</a>
(credits, magazine scores, compilations and the sequel, Empire Of Karn).

[Fact:commodore:Born On The Commodore 64]
Heroes Of Karn was written for the Commodore 64 first, and the Spectrum
version is a conversion. Reviewers noted that the Commodore original used
LIST to show the inventory where the Spectrum version uses I, that the
parser was tidied up on the way across, and that the Spectrum has fewer
pictures than the Commodore 64 but better ones, drawn by Terry Greer. See the
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=6431"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">contemporary reviews</a>.

[Fact:credits:Credits]
The game was designed by Ian Gray and coded by David M. Banner, with
graphics, inlay and loading screen by Terry Greer. It sold for &pound;5.50.
Source: <a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/6431/ZX-Spectrum/Heroes_of_Karn"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Spectrum Computing</a>.

[Fact:heroes:The Four Heroes]
The heroes you have to rescue are Beren, Istar the Wise, Haldir the
Elf-minstrel and the dwarf Khadim, and Anton the Gypsy King also turns up.
Sinclair User pointed out that the first hero has been turned into a frog,
and is changed back "in the time-honoured way". See the
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=6431"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">reviews</a>.

[Fact:covertapes:Compilations And Covertapes]
The game reappeared on Beau-Jolly's 6 Computer Hits compilation in 1985, and
was given away on the Your Sinclair issue 48 Smash Tape in December 1989.
Source: <a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/6431/ZX-Spectrum/Heroes_of_Karn"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Spectrum Computing</a>.

[Fact:engine:The Interceptor Engine]
Heroes Of Karn shares most of its engine with the other Interceptor
adventures by David M. Banner. Its routines are cross-referenced to the
matching code in Message From Andromeda, Forest At World's End, The Jewels
Of Babylon, Warlord and After Shock throughout this disassembly.

[Fact:leftovers:Leftovers]
The game has a few bits it never uses: a spare <code>NOP</code> at
#R$B373(#N$B3A5), probably where an instruction was patched out; the pointers
at #R$AEEC and #R$AEEE are never set and the scenic-event count at #R$AEF8
stays at #N($00), so the code that reads them at #R$B373(#N$B3A6) and
#R$B714(#N$B71C) does nothing; and the engine's own copy of "I don't see the
point." at #R$B17A is never printed, because the game uses a second copy at
#R$D45A instead.
